CHGRP(1)                       P32W Unix                        CHGRP(1)

NAME
       chgrp - change the group of files

SYNOPSIS
       chgrp [-R] group file ...

DESCRIPTION
       chgrp makes group the group of each file; the owner stays as
       it was. group is a name from /etc/group or a numeric group
       id (a name is looked up first; a string of digits that is
       not a name is taken as a number). An unknown name is an
       error and no file is changed.

       Only root may change a group: chgrp uses chown(2), which is
       root's alone, as in Unix V7. Anyone else gets
       chgrp: file: not owner (only root may change a group). A
       file that already has the group asked for is left alone,
       without error, so anyone may do that.

OPTIONS
       -R     also change everything inside each directory, and
              inside the directories in it, and so on. Each
              directory is changed before its contents.

EXAMPLES
       chgrp staff /usr/src/notes     give a file to the staff group
       chgrp -R staff /usr/src/cc     a whole tree
       chgrp 10 report                by number

FILES
       /etc/group     group names and ids

EXIT STATUS
       0 if every file was changed; 1 if group is unknown or if any
       file could not be changed (chgrp says why and goes on); 2
       for a usage error.

SEE ALSO
       chown(1), chmod(1), ls(1), id(1), chown(2), group(5)

BUGS
       An ordinary user cannot give their own file to one of their
       groups, as later systems allow. With -R, a directory that
       cannot be changed is not entered.
